WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Practical GPU cost control starts before a workload runs.

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:12.000
Classify the workload, define its cost intent, assign ownership, and choose controls that match the value and risk of the work.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.200
Experiments, training jobs, batch evaluations, and production inference do not need identical policies.

4
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.200
Choose the smallest suitable resource, measure utilization, and scale deliberately.

5
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:28.400
Use queues, schedules, shutdown policies, timeouts, retry limits, and cleanup automation to reduce idle time and runaway execution.

6
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.000
Revisit right-sizing as models, data, and traffic change.

7
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.800
Manage the costs around the GPU as carefully as the GPU itself.

8
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:43.600
Storage, checkpoints, logs, artifacts, snapshots, caches, data movement, failed jobs, and operational labor all affect effective cost.

9
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.800
Hidden spend becomes easier to control when ownership and retention rules are explicit.

10
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.200
Finally, make cost visible and reviewable.

11
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:58.800
Budgets, tags, quotas, guardrails, dashboards, anomaly alerts, forecasts, and regular reviews help teams connect engineering decisions to financial outcomes.

12
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.800
Sustainable savings come from repeatable habits, not one-time cleanup.

13
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:09.600
When every workload has intent, ownership, limits, and review, cloud GPU cost becomes manageable instead of mysterious.
